{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0013","statement":"The parsing model uses three types of input representation: multilingual word clusters and embeddings, token-level language information, and language-specific features (fine-grained POS tags).","source_span":"(i) multilingual word clusters and embeddings; (ii) token-level language information; and (iii) language-specific features (fine-grained POS tags)"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0013","statement":"The proposed model is named \"Many Languages, One Parser\" (MaLOPa) because it trains one parsing model on the union of treebanks in the source languages.","source_span":"hence the name “Many Languages, One Parser” (MaLOPa)"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0013","statement":"In the small treebank setup with 3,000 token annotations, the parser outperforms a strong monolingual baseline by 5.7 absolute LAS points per language, on average.","source_span":"In the small treebank setup with 3,000 token annotations, we show that our parser consistently outperforms a strong monolingual baseline with 5.7 absolute LAS (labeled attachment score) points per language, on average."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0013","statement":"The language-universal parser extends the stack LSTM (S-LSTM) parser of Dyer et al. (2015).","source_span":"which extends the stack LSTM (S-LSTM) parser of dyer:15"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0013","statement":"The parser described in the paper is publicly available.","source_span":"Our parser is publicly available."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0013","statement":"The parser is evaluated in three data scenarios: a large treebank in the target language (Table 3), a small treebank (Table 7), or no treebank (Table 8).","source_span":"when the target language has a large treebank (Table 3 ), a small treebank (Table 7 ), or no treebank (Table 8 )"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0013","fact_refs":[1],"question":"What does the acronym MaLOPa stand for, and what is the core idea behind it?","answer":"MaLOPa stands for \"Many Languages, One Parser\" — the idea of training a single parsing model on the union of treebanks from multiple source languages instead of a separate monolingual parser per language."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0013","fact_refs":[3],"question":"Which existing parser architecture does the MaLOPa model extend?","answer":"It extends the stack LSTM (S-LSTM) transition-based parser introduced by Dyer et al. (2015)."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0013","fact_refs":[2],"question":"In the low-resource setting with 3,000 token annotations, how much better does the multilingual parser perform than a monolingual baseline?","answer":"It outperforms a strong monolingual baseline by 5.7 absolute LAS (labeled attachment score) points per language, on average."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0013","fact_refs":[0],"question":"What three types of input features does the MaLOPa parsing model use?","answer":"It uses (i) multilingual word clusters and embeddings, (ii) token-level language information, and (iii) language-specific features such as fine-grained POS tags."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0013","fact_refs":[4],"question":"Is the MaLOPa parser publicly released?","answer":"Yes, the paper states that the parser is publicly available."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0013","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What three data scenarios does the paper use to evaluate the parser?","answer":"A large treebank in the target language (Table 3), a small treebank (Table 7), and no treebank at all in the target language (Table 8)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0014","statement":"Charles Hockett identified duality of patterning as one of the core design features of human language in a seminal paper.","source_span":"In a seminal paper, Charles Hockett BIBREF0 identified duality of patterning as one of the core design features of human language."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0014","statement":"Combinatoriality is the property whereby meaningless forms (phonemes) are combined into meaningful units.","source_span":"meaningless forms (typically referred to as phonemes) are combined into meaningful units (henceforth this property will be referred to as combinatoriality)"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0014","statement":"Compositionality is the property whereby meaningful units (morphemes) are composed into larger units whose meaning relates to the meaning of the composing units.","source_span":"meaningful units (typically referred to as morphemes) are composed into larger units, the meaning of which is related to the individual meaning of the composing units (henceforth this property will be referred to as compositionality)"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0014","statement":"The English forms /k/, /a/, and /t/ combine in different orders to produce the three unrelated words /kat/ ('cat'), /akt/ ('act'), and /tak/ ('tack').","source_span":"the English forms /k/, /a/, and /t/ are combined in different ways to obtain the three words /kat/, /akt/, and /tak/ (respectively written 'cat', 'act' and 'tack')"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0014","statement":"All the many words of the English lexicon can be obtained by combinations of about forty phonemes.","source_span":"all of the many words of the English lexicon can be obtained by relatively simple combinations of about forty phonemes"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0014","statement":"The paper's multi-agent modeling scheme for the emergence of duality of patterning is called the Blending Game.","source_span":"the theoretical predictions of a multi-agents modeling scheme, namely the Blending Game, are in surprisingly good agreement with empirical data"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0014","statement":"The Blending Game model has two parameters: the graph connectivity p_link and the memory scale tau.","source_span":"as a function of the two parameters of the model, the graph connectivity $p_{link}$ and the memory scale $\\tau $"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0014","statement":"Evidence suggests humans can evolve languages with duality of patterning within only one or two generations, as seen in Nicaraguan Sign Language and the emergence of Pidgin and Creole languages.","source_span":"there is evidence suggesting that humans are capable of evolving languages with duality of patterning in the course of only one or two generations (consider for instance Nicaraguan Sign Language BIBREF10 or the emergence of Pidgins and Creole languages BIBREF11 )"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0014","fact_refs":[0],"question":"Who first identified duality of patterning as a core design feature of human language?","answer":"Charles Hockett, in a seminal paper."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0014","fact_refs":[1,2],"question":"What is the difference between combinatoriality and compositionality as defined in this paper?","answer":"Combinatoriality is the combination of meaningless forms (phonemes) into meaningful units, while compositionality is the composition of meaningful units (morphemes) into larger units whose meaning relates to the meaning of their parts."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0014","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What example does the paper give to illustrate combinatoriality using English phonemes?","answer":"The forms /k/, /a/, and /t/ combine in different orders to form the unrelated words 'cat' (/kat/), 'act' (/akt/), and 'tack' (/tak/)."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0014","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What multi-agent modeling scheme does the paper use to explain the emergence of duality of patterning?","answer":"The Blending Game, a model in which a population of individuals plays language games aiming at success in communication."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0014","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What are the two parameters of the Blending Game model?","answer":"The graph connectivity parameter p_link and the memory scale parameter tau."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0014","fact_refs":[7],"question":"What real-world examples does the paper cite to suggest duality of patterning can emerge within just one or two generations?","answer":"Nicaraguan Sign Language and the emergence of Pidgin and Creole languages."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0015","statement":"CLSTM (Contextual LSTM) is an extension of the LSTM model that incorporates contextual features such as topics.","source_span":"we present CLSTM (Contextual LSTM), an extension of the recurrent neural network LSTM (Long-Short Term Memory) model, where we incorporate contextual features (e.g., topics) into the model"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0015","statement":"CLSTM is evaluated on three NLP tasks: word prediction, next sentence selection, and sentence topic prediction.","source_span":"We evaluate CLSTM on three specific NLP tasks: word prediction, next sentence selection, and sentence topic prediction."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0015","statement":"On the next sentence selection task, CLSTM achieves relative accuracy improvements of 21% on the Wikipedia dataset and 18% on the Google News dataset over baseline LSTM.","source_span":"on the next sentence selection task, we get relative accuracy improvements of 21% for the Wikipedia dataset and 18% for the Google News dataset"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0015","statement":"The CLSTM and baseline LSTM models were trained on two corpora: English documents in Wikipedia and a subset of articles from a recent snapshot of English Google News.","source_span":"Results from experiments run on two corpora, English documents in Wikipedia and a subset of articles from a recent snapshot of English Google News"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0015","statement":"The vocabulary sizes used were 130K words for the Wikipedia dataset and 100K words for the Google News dataset.","source_span":"The vocabulary we handled in the modeling here was also large: 130K words for Wikipedia, 100K for Google news."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0015","statement":"RNN-based language models were proposed by Mikolov et al., and the LSTM variant of RNN-LMs was introduced by Sundermeyer et al.","source_span":"RNN-based language models (RNN-LMs) were proposed by Mikolov et al. BIBREF17 , and in particular the variant using LSTMs was introduced by Sundermeyer et al. BIBREF18"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0015","fact_refs":[0],"question":"What does CLSTM stand for and what does it add to the standard LSTM model?","answer":"CLSTM stands for Contextual LSTM — it extends the standard LSTM language model by incorporating contextual features such as topics."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0015","fact_refs":[1],"question":"On which three NLP tasks is the CLSTM model evaluated in this paper?","answer":"Word prediction, next sentence selection, and sentence topic prediction."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0015","fact_refs":[2],"question":"How much did CLSTM improve accuracy over the baseline LSTM on the next sentence selection task, and on which datasets?","answer":"CLSTM achieved a 21% relative accuracy improvement on the Wikipedia dataset and an 18% improvement on the Google News dataset compared to the baseline LSTM."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0015","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What two large-scale corpora were used to train the CLSTM and baseline LSTM models?","answer":"English documents in Wikipedia and a subset of articles from a recent snapshot of English Google News."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0015","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What vocabulary sizes were used for the Wikipedia and Google News datasets in this study?","answer":"130,000 words for Wikipedia and 100,000 words for Google News."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0015","fact_refs":[5],"question":"Who proposed RNN-based language models, and who introduced the LSTM variant of them?","answer":"Mikolov et al. proposed RNN-based language models, and Sundermeyer et al. introduced the LSTM variant of RNN-LMs."}
